From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Size the rdram reservation for the target's address space

    constexpr size_t allocation_size = 4096ULL * 1024ULL * 1024ULL;

On a 32-bit target size_t is 32 bits wide, so this does not merely become an
unreasonably large number -- it evaluates to exactly zero. VirtualAlloc is then
asked to reserve nothing, fails, and the program stops at "Failed to allocate
memory" before drawing a frame. It compiles, it links, and it dies at startup.

GCC does say so, but only under -Woverflow and only as a warning among the
thousands a generated-code build produces:

    warning: conversion from 'long long unsigned int' to 'size_t'
    {aka 'unsigned int'} changes value from '4294967296' to '0'

The two sizes become target-dependent. The values for a 32-bit target are not
chosen to make the numbers fit but to keep the mechanism intact: the reservation
exists so that an out-of-range guest address lands on protected pages and faults
immediately, rather than quietly reading whatever is mapped. Committing 4 MiB
and reserving 8 MiB leaves 4 MiB of protected space above the rdram, so an
invalid access still traps.

The first version committed 4 MiB, sized against the *game's* rdram: DKR's main
pool stops at RAM_END, 0x80400000, and the decomp sets EXPANSION_PAK_SUPPORT to
0. That reasoning was right about the game and wrong about librecomp, which
places its own regions far above the game's -- PI handles at 0x80800000, the
patch region just after, the mod region at 0x81000000 -- and init_heap() puts the
recompiled heap at mod_rdram_start, 16 MiB in.

With 4 MiB committed and 8 MiB reserved that write landed outside the reservation
altogether. Observed on Windows 95 with the real ROM: an access violation in
o1heapInit, inside the loop that clears the instance bins, i.e. on the very first
byte of the heap.

The layout is left alone and the sizes follow it: 20 MiB committed puts the heap
16 MiB in with 4 MiB of usable arena, 24 MiB reserved keeps 4 MiB of protected
space above so an out-of-range guest address still traps. The target has 47 MiB
free (ADR 0003), so this is affordable. A static_assert now forbids dropping back
below mod_rdram_start.

Verified on the target: the scheme works at this size, and reserving is the
binding constraint rather than committing -- that machine tops out at 1 GiB of
reservation and 256 MiB of read-write commit.

The 64-bit values are untouched and still evaluate to 536870912 and 4294967296;
all 26 translation units still compile there.

diff --git a/librecomp/include/librecomp/addresses.hpp b/librecomp/include/librecomp/addresses.hpp
index b1b50cb..0372074 100644
--- a/librecomp/include/librecomp/addresses.hpp
+++ b/librecomp/include/librecomp/addresses.hpp
@@ -2,14 +2,62 @@
 #define __RECOMP_ADDRESSES_HPP__
 
 #include <cstdint>
+#include <cstddef>
 #include "ultramodern/ultra64.h"
 #include "recomp.h"
 
 namespace recomp {
-    // 512GB (kseg0 size)
+    // How much rdram is committed, and how much address space is reserved
+    // around it.
+    //
+    // The reservation exists so that an out-of-range guest address lands on
+    // protected pages and faults immediately, instead of quietly reading
+    // whatever happens to be mapped. The defaults are generous because a
+    // 64-bit host has address space to spare.
+    //
+    // A 32-bit host does not, and the defaults are worse than merely large
+    // there: size_t is 32 bits wide, so 4096 * 1024 * 1024 does not overflow
+    // into something big -- it evaluates to exactly zero. VirtualAlloc is then
+    // asked for a zero-byte reservation, fails, and the program stops at
+    // "Failed to allocate memory". The compiler does warn, but only with
+    // -Woverflow, and only as a warning.
+    //
+    // The sizes are therefore target-dependent. On a 32-bit target they are
+    // chosen to keep the mechanism intact rather than to disable it: 4 MiB
+    // committed, 8 MiB reserved, so 4 MiB of protected space still sits above
+    // the rdram and still traps.
+#if SIZE_MAX > 0xFFFFFFFFu
+    // 512MB (kseg0 size)
     constexpr size_t mem_size = 512ULL * 1024ULL * 1024ULL;
     // 4GB (the full address space)
     constexpr size_t allocation_size = 4096ULL * 1024ULL * 1024ULL;
+#else
+    // 4 MiB was sized against the *game's* rdram: DKR's pool stops at RAM_END,
+    // 0x80400000, and the decomp never uses the Expansion Pak. That reasoning
+    // was right about the game and wrong about librecomp, which places its own
+    // regions far above the game's:
+    //
+    //     0x80800000  PI handles          8 MiB in
+    //     0x80801000  patch region
+    //     0x81000000  mod region         16 MiB in
+    //
+    // and init_heap() puts the recompiled heap at mod_rdram_start. With 4 MiB
+    // committed and 8 MiB reserved, that write lands outside the reservation
+    // altogether. The observed failure on Windows 95 was an access violation in
+    // o1heapInit, inside the loop that clears the instance bins -- that is, on
+    // the very first byte of the heap.
+    //
+    // The layout is left alone and the sizes follow it instead: 20 MiB committed
+    // puts the heap 16 MiB in with 4 MiB of usable arena, and 24 MiB reserved
+    // keeps 4 MiB of protected space above so an out-of-range guest address
+    // still traps. The target has 47 MiB free (ADR 0003), so this is affordable.
+    constexpr size_t mem_size = 20ULL * 1024ULL * 1024ULL;
+    constexpr size_t allocation_size = 24ULL * 1024ULL * 1024ULL;
+    static_assert(allocation_size > mem_size,
+        "the reservation must exceed the committed rdram, or nothing traps");
+    static_assert(mem_size > 0x01000000ULL,
+        "the committed rdram must reach mod_rdram_start, where the heap begins");
+#endif
     // We need a place in rdram to hold the PI handles, so pick an address in extended rdram
     constexpr int32_t cart_handle = 0x80800000;
     constexpr int32_t drive_handle = (int32_t)(cart_handle + sizeof(OSPiHandle));
